hi there steve kaufman here  i want to continue on the subject of  corrections  because it seems to generate quite a bit  of interest from  people commenting here at my youtube  channel and people say why are you so  sensitive about people who correct you  and how will people ever improve if they  aren't corrected  and these kinds of things now i  apologize that on these rants of mine i  don't have my thoughts necessarily  always  well organized because it's all i can do  to find the time to sit down here and  talk in front of the video so i'm going  to ramble a bit here but  the common question is number one  uh why is it that you're so sensitive  why don't you like to be  corrected  well  when i think about it  uh  i like doing things in language learning  that are meaningful i like to listen to  things that interest me  and when i speak to people i want it to  be a meaningful conversation i don't  like pretending that i'm at the bank at  the train station in a restaurant role  playing  none of that stuff appeals to me  when i start speaking to people as i'm  doing now in romanian  we're talking about  okay how was your day how was work but  then we're talking about  romanian food we're talking about  romanian history romanian geography  present-day romanian politics things  that interest me so i'm totally into  those conversations  and to me the important thing about  speaking and and output  is that that you are doing it it's the  act of of immersing yourself in the  language using the language that is the  most important so while i'm doing this  and searching for words  uh if i'm corrected i find it quite  off-putting so i don't like it for that  reason  and i think most people if they were  honest  don't like it unless they're in a  classroom where they feel they've paid  the instructor  to correct them and they're not getting  their money's worth if the instructor  doesn't correct them  but if i meet someone here in  my daily life in vancouver  uh who is let's say an immigrant and  we're having a conversation let's say a  salesman comes to me this happens a  salesman comes and doesn't speak perfect  english or somebody that i deal with  at the store or  somewhere doesn't speak perfect english  it would never cross my mind to correct  them they're operating in the language  you know it's like at a restaurant if  i'm at a korean restaurant and the  prayer the waitress speaks to me or any  you know  restaurant that speaks to me in english  and i'll correct them no that's not how  you say it i mean that's you just don't  do that because it's off-putting when  people are genuinely trying to  communicate so that's why i don't like  it  uh  to me the conversation the big thing  about the conversation is that it's it's  an opportunity for more input i'm  listening to the native speaker and i'm  listening for the words that the native  speaker uses and at times when i search  for words the native speaker will  volunteer the words i'm learning the  words  and as content a conversation is  tremendously powerful it's it has it's  what i call high resonance because  you're in that discussion and you're  searching for words and maybe the native  speaker provides the words  uh and you're listening to the words  that they're using so it's very high  resonance very effective input and it's  a prac it's an opportunity to practice  output but as much as that or more so  it's a tremendously rich input  opportunity and so the more i speak the  more the native speaker speaks the more  natural and flowing our conversation the  more i benefit the more i'm immersed  language learning is more about  immersion  than  correction or perfection  so that was the first one why do i not  like being corrected second comment i  get is how are you ever going to improve  how are people going to improve if you  don't correct them well my  take on that is how do you know that  correcting them improves them that's the  whole point we have no proof the  assumption is that correcting people  improves them  i think that that's not true at all  because  in any language there is so much to  learn it's not just  the tremendous variety of structures and  forms that we can use it's even the  words themselves the phrases themselves  phrasal verbs how many phrasal verbs are  there in english uh  the fact that a word  that you looked up in the dictionary may  not have in fact the same meaning or  being may not be used the same way in  another language there's an almost  infinite variety of things you cannot  learn all of those things based on  feedback and instruction from a native  speaker those are all things that you  have to acquire yourself naturally  through a lot of exposure and when you  first start out and if you read you know  teach yourself or whatever they  introduce some concepts  90 of them are over your head you can't  deal with these things  at first but as you start to become  familiar with some elements now you can  go back again and look at some of the  other elements that were introduced that  are over your head and so gradually  as you gain more and more confidence and  as long as you remain motivated  to improve  then you will improve essentially on  your own not because people correct you  people cannot possibly correct you for  all of the issues that you're going to  come across in the language in fact the  number of things they can correct is  very small  and the things that they will tend to  correct very often are things that are  very  tenacious as errors  uh we've often mentioned this third  person singular present tense in english  he go people say he go instead of he  goes i've often talked about  chinese people  they continue to say my husband she  because in chinese they don't have in  speaking a difference between he and she  correcting them is not going to change  that at some point they have to start  noticing it and it has to become part of  the way they use the language so  i don't think that correcting  people  in fact improves them it may have a  minor effect primarily people have to do  it on their own so the question how will  they ever improve if you don't correct  them they do not improve because you  correct them they improve if they put  the time and effort into  uh correcting themselves and  those people who have been the most  corrected in classrooms and so forth uh  esl classes they often speak the worst  and people who have been out exposing  themselves to the language naturally  they often speak  better  but still you have to be motivated to  try to improve so if i speak to uh  you know there's a vietnamese gardener  that comes around  our house once every two weeks i mean  his he he speaks a lot uh but he's never  made a particularly  uh you know concerted effort to work on  his language if i were to correct him  when he speaks it would have no impact  on him whatsoever none zero uh if on the  other hand he undertook to do a lot of  reading and listening and focusing in on  those structures that he had difficulty  with  he might improve i had made some notes  here i think that's about  uh yeah  another part of this is  uh  language learning has to come from  outside in because you don't have the  language in you and it comes from  overwhelmingly from input all right when  you're talking about correction  you're talking about  output  first and foremost because if i'm not  outputting anything you can't correct me  the major part of language learning  takes place  when we are ingesting taking the  language in because we don't have the  language in ourselves the language has  to come from  what native speakers have written  or said  so that the the main thrust in language  learning is input and contrary to what  many people seem to believe i do not  believe  that the doing part role playing  speaking answering questions drills all  of that  is  where you learn the language you can do  those things or not do them  but you learn the language by hearing it  and seeing it and noticing it  and you don't have to do a lot of  speaking  an example is  by romanian overwhelmingly i just listen  and read and  yeah i mean when i get to romania i will  do a video in cebu i'm going to be in  cebu on the 8th and 9th of june and  there's an international theater  festival there so i'm going to try and  shoot some video while i'm in cebu and  i'm going to speak some romanian but i  haven't spoken very much  because the language has to come from  outside and so when you do speak the  main thing is to immerse yourself in  speaking  just communicating listening to what the  other person is  saying  and don't turn these opportunities for  communication into classroom sessions  into sterile classroom sessions with a  lot of correction it doesn't help  research says it doesn't help my  experience says it doesn't help but i  will say that if that's what you like if  you like to be corrected by all means  ask people to correct you but if people  don't ask to be corrected you shouldn't  correct them  so  one more  rant on the issue of corrections i look  forward to your comments  bye for now

